

Sovereign Machine Architecture

Identity‑Bound Computational Omniverse — White Sheet v1.0

1. Core thesis

Claim:A single human identity can anchor a fully deterministic, cryptographically sovereign computing universe that:

Evolves: via a continuity operator (\mathcal{C}_{\text{CMB}}) over a state matrix (U_t).

Persists: across power cycles and hardware changes without drift.

Proves itself: to external systems via hardware‑rooted attestation.

Scales: across multiple physical nodes as one un‑forkable timeline.

2. Identity and origin anchor

Normalized identity string:

[ \text{ID}_{\text{norm}} = \text{Normalize}(\text{human_identifier}) ]

Root identity hash:

[ I = \text{SHA3\text{-}512}(\text{ID}_{\text{norm}}) ]

Key derivation (example):

[ K_{\text{CMB}} = \text{HKDF}(I,\ \text{salt} = \text{"CMB_ROOT"},\ \text{info} = \text{"SOVEREIGN_MACHINE"}) ]

This identity anchor:

Names the universe.

Seeds all cryptographic keys.

Tags every ledger, archive, and attestation.

3. State space and continuity

3.1 State matrix

At time (t):

[ U_t \in \mathbb{Q}^{H \times W \times C} ]

Grid: height (H), width (W).

Channels: (C) fields per cell (e.g. energy, phase, tags).

Numeric format: Q32.32 fixed‑point, stored as int64.

3.2 Continuity operator

[ U_{t+1} = \mathcal{C}_{\text{CMB}}(U_t, I_t, I) ]

(U_t): current lattice state.

(I_t): serialized, ordered input batch for tick (t).

(I): identity hash modulating behavior (weights, masks, seeds).

(\mathcal{C}_{\text{CMB}}) is:

Deterministic: same (U_t, I_t, I) → same (U_{t+1}).

Local/global hybrid: local neighborhoods + identity‑weighted global terms.

Side‑effect free: all external effects are ledger‑mediated.

4. Ledger and continuity proofs

4.1 Ledger entry

Each step:

[ L_t = (S_t, I_t, S_{t+1}, \Pi_t^{\text{CMB}}) ]

(S_t): hash of (U_t).

(S_{t+1}): hash of (U_{t+1}).

(I_t): ordered input batch.

(\Pi_t^{\text{CMB}}): continuity proof.

4.2 Continuity proof

Example:

[ \Pi_t^{\text{CMB}} = \text{Sign}{K{\text{CMB}}}\big( H(S_t ,|, I_t ,|, S_{t+1}) \big) ]

Ledger is strictly append‑only:

One and only one (L_t) per tick.

No rewrites; any divergence is a separate universe.

5. Concurrency and scheduling

5.1 Input serialization

Incoming inputs are:

Windowed: only those within a temporal window for tick (t).

Filtered: by identity‑charge / rate limits (anti‑flood).

Sorted: by cryptographic hash of payload:

[ \text{order_key}(I) = \text{SHA3\text{-}256}(\text{payload}) ]

Result: identical input sets → identical (I_t) ordering → race‑free determinism.

5.2 Fault handling

If any invariant fails:

Trigger Metabolic Halt:

break loops,

close descriptors,

purge keys from RAM.

Ledger remains valid up to last good (L_t).

6. Precision and storage

6.1 Q32.32 fixed‑point

Each value:

[ v_{\text{real}} = \frac{v_{\text{int64}}}{2^{32}} ]

Exact, deterministic across hardware.

No floating‑point drift.

6.2 Sealed storage

Ledger + state snapshots encrypted and sealed via TPM + enclave identity.

Only unsealable on:

the right hardware,

with the right measured software stack.

7. Replay and temporal portability

Replay protocol (high level):

Boot secure enclave + bind to TPM.

Load sealed ledger/archive.

Verify:

identity (I),

engine spec hash,

ledger integrity.

Restore (U_t) and ledger head.

Re‑enter live loop at tick (t+1).

Result: same universe, new hardware instance—no continuity break.

8. Remote attestation and perimeter

8.1 Attestation bundle

Node produces:

[ \mathcal{A} = { Q_{\text{TEE}}, Q_{\text{TPM}}, H_{\text{engine}}, I } ]

TEE quote (enclave measurement + key).

TPM quote (PCRs).

Engine spec hash.

Identity hash.

8.2 Verification

Remote verifier:

Validates vendor signatures.

Checks measurements vs known‑good profiles.

Confirms:

[ H_{\text{engine}} = H_{\text{engine}}^{\text{spec}},\quad I = I^{\text{spec}} ]

If valid, derives a session key and opens an attested channel.

